<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carita Parahyangan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 16th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Carita Parahyangan is an Old Sundanese palm-leaf <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a>, usually dated to the late 16th century. It narrates the history of the Sundanese kingdoms of Galuh and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunda-strait">Sunda</a> from the 7th century to the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakuan-pajajaran">Pakuan Pajajaran</a>, listing kings and the lengths of their reigns. Though it mixes legend with history, it is one of the main sources for pre-Islamic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-java">West Java</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The text survives in a single <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a>, registered as Kropak 406, which came from the collection of the Bataviaasch Genootschap voor Kunsten en Wetenschappen (Batavian Society of Arts and Sciences) and is now kept in the Perpustakaan Nasional, the National Library of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jakarta">Jakarta</a>. It consists of 47 leaves of lontar palm leaf, each about 21 by 3 cm, with four lines of Old Sundanese script on every leaf. Its name comes from the Parahyangan highlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-java">West Java</a>, from Sundanese words meaning the abode of the hyangs, or gods.</p><p>The chronicle follows the Sunda Kingdom from the Galuh period, in the time of Wretikandayun and King Sanjaya, down to the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakuan-pajajaran">Pakuan Pajajaran</a>, the Sundanese capital, when it was invaded by the Banten Sultanate with help from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirebon">Cirebon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demak-sultanate">Demak Sultanates</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> holds two texts. The larger is the Carita Parahyangan proper, an account of Sundanese kings and kingdoms of the pre-Islamic period. The second, which its modern editor called the Fragmen Carita Parahyangan, fills 13 leaves, or 25 pages, and differs in its hand: it has an irregular number of lines, from three to six a page, and small differences of script. It contains three main stories of rulers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunda-strait">Sunda</a> kingdom at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakuan-pajajaran">Pakuan Pajajaran</a>, and its second, larger part is of interest for social and economic history. Scholars hold that the two texts were brought together in one manuscript at an early stage.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The scholar Holle identified the text under this name in 1882, and it was published by Holle that year and by Pleyte in 1914; Poerbatjaraka later gave a complete transliteration. In 1962 the scholar Noorduyn restored the order of the leaves, which had fallen into disarray, and published an annotated transliteration and translation of the first part, adding corrections in 1966 after rereading the original.</p><p>Working from Noorduyn's order of the leaves, Atja and Danasasmita published a new transliteration with an Indonesian translation and notes in 1981. In 1995 Darsa and Ekadjati produced a further edition and translation, which printed the Fragment for the first time, and in 1999 Darsa examined how the two texts of Kropak 406 relate to each other.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Carita Parahyangan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Carita Parahyangan</b>, a Sundanese text of the late 16th century, makes Sanjaya Sanna's son, tells how Sanna was defeated by Purbasora of Galuh and how Sanjaya avenged him and ruled West, Central and East Java and Bali; romanticised as it is, its agreement with the Canggal inscription…</blockquote><a href="/empire/medang-kingdom">Medang Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>According to Sundanese tradition, chiefly the 16th-century <b>Carita Parahyangan</b>, Galuh emerged around 669 when the lands of Tarumanagara divided along the Citarum River, Galuh taking the east and the Sunda Kingdom the west.</blockquote><a href="/state/galuh-kingdom">Galuh Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
